Yes. Before git add ., create a .gitignore file so Git does not upload unwanted files such as virtual environments, databases, cache files, models, and node modules.

Run:

Shell
cat > .gitignore << 'EOF'
# Python
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
 
# Database
*.db
security.db
 
# ML Models
*.pkl
*.joblib
 
# Logs
*.log
 
# Environment variables
.env
 
# Node / React
node_modules/
build/
 
# VS Code
.vscode/
 
# IntelliJ
.idea/
 
# OS Files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
 
# Kubernetes temp files
*.swp
 
# Coverage
.coverage
htmlcov/
 
# pytest
.pytest_cache/
 
# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/
 
# Docker
docker-compose.override.yml
EOF

Verify:

Shell
cat .gitignore

Then:

Shell
git status

If you already added files before creating .gitignore, refresh Git:

Shell
git rm -r --cached .
git add .

Now commit and push:

Shell
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit - Security Incident AI Platform"
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

For your project specifically, the most important files to ignore are:

Plain Text
venv/
node_modules/
security.db
model.pkl
__pycache__/
.env

Otherwise your repository can become hundreds of MB in size and may accidentally upload local databases, trained models, or secrets.

